---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Stage from "../../components/Stage.astro";
import OneColour from "../../components/OneColour.astro";
import tokens from "@intervolutions/ivolt/tokens.json";
const val = (k, mode) => { const v = tokens[k].value; return typeof v === "string" ? v : v[mode]; };
const semantic = Object.entries(tokens).filter(([k]) => k.startsWith("--iv-color-") && !k.startsWith("--iv-palette"));
---
<Base title="Design tokens, dark mode and themes" description="iVOLT design tokens: palette, semantic colours, spacing, type, radius, shadow, focus, motion and breakpoints, with light, dark and system themes.">
  <article class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Tokens and themes</h1>
    <p>Every visual decision is a custom property prefixed <code>--iv-</code>, generated from one JSON source. Override any of them on <code>:root</code> or on a scope; nothing needs recompiling.</p>
<pre><code>:root &#123;
  --iv-color-primary: #4338CA;      /* your brand */
  --iv-color-on-primary: #FFFFFF;
  --iv-radius-md: 0.25rem;
&#125;
.checkout &#123; --iv-button-radius: var(--iv-radius-full); &#125;  /* component-local */</code></pre>

    <OneColour lang="en" />

    <h2 id="palettes">Where the mixing happens, and palettes</h2>
    <p><span class="docs-new">New in 1.1</span> The ten derived tokens are declared once, as <code>color-mix(in oklab, var(--iv-color-primary), var(--iv-mix-&lt;name&gt;))</code>, on <code>:root</code>, on every <code>[data-iv-theme]</code> scope and on every <code>[data-iv-palette]</code> scope. That is where you set the primary: on <code>:root</code> for the whole site, or on one of those scopes for a part of it. On any other element the derived tokens are already computed by the time they are inherited, so changing the primary on a plain <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> recolours the primary itself but not its hover or its gradient — add <code>data-iv-palette</code> (with no value) to that element and it re-mixes them. The defaults resolve to the 1.0 values within ΔE2000 &lt; 1, checked in a browser on every build, so a site that never touches the primary sees no change.</p>
    <p><code>data-iv-palette</code> with a value points the primary of a subtree at another role of the theme: <code>accent</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code>, <code>danger</code>, <code>info</code> or <code>neutral</code> (the text colour on the page colour). The primary and the ink drawn on it change together, and every derived token follows — a danger zone in a settings page, a success panel, a monochrome footer, with one attribute and no new classes.</p>
    <Stage name="theme/one-colour" title="Default cobalt, one variable, and data-iv-palette=&quot;success&quot;" />
    <p><code>--iv-color-on-primary</code>, the colour of text drawn on the primary, is not derived: CSS in the browsers iVOLT supports cannot pick a contrasting colour. Set it with the primary and check the pair; the measured figures above show how much room each sample colour leaves. The recipes themselves are public tokens, one per derived value and per theme, in the form <code>&lt;colour&gt; &lt;percentage&gt;</code>; they can be tuned, and their values may be refined in a minor release:</p>
    <div class="docs-scroller">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Recipe</th><th>Light</th><th>Dark</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-primary-hover</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-hover", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-hover", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-primary-active</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-active", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-active", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-primary-subtle</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-subtle", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-subtle", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-primary-border</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-border", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-primary-border", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-focus</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-focus", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-focus", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-focus-halo</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-focus-halo", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-focus-halo", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-glow-primary</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-glow-primary", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-glow-primary", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-glow-secondary</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-glow-secondary", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-glow-secondary", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-brand-start</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-brand-start", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-brand-start", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-brand-end</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-brand-end", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-brand-end", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-gradient-start</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-gradient-start", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-gradient-start", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-mix-gradient-end</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-gradient-end", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-mix-gradient-end", "dark")}</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A theme of your own can also change a recipe: <code>--iv-mix-brand-end: var(--iv-color-accent) 35%</code> ends both gradients a third of the way to your accent, which is what the presets of the <a href="/themes">theme gallery</a> do.</p>

    <h2>Themes</h2>
    <p><code>data-iv-theme</code> accepts <code>light</code> (default when absent), <code>dark</code> and <code>system</code>. Scopes nest and the nearest explicit value wins; <code>system</code> follows <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> without JavaScript or storage.</p>
    <Stage primary name="theme/nested" title="Nested scopes" />
    <p>The optional <code>theme</code> module adds <code>getTheme()</code>, <code>setTheme(theme, &#123; persist &#125;)</code>, <code>resolveTheme()</code> and <code>restoreTheme()</code>, and dispatches <code>iv:themechange</code> on <code>document</code>. Persistence is opt-in. To avoid a flash before the stored theme applies, add a tiny inline script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>; with a strict CSP allow it by hash or nonce.</p>

    <h3>Switching with a view transition</h3>
    <p><span class="docs-new">New in 1.1</span> <code>@intervolutions/ivolt/theme-transition</code> exports <code>setThemeWithTransition(theme, options)</code>: the same call as <code>setTheme</code>, wrapped in <code>document.startViewTransition</code> where the browser has it and the reader has not asked for less motion, so the page cross-fades between themes instead of flipping. Elsewhere it is <code>setTheme</code> as it was. It returns a promise of the resolved theme, fires <code>iv:themechange</code> once the browser has taken its snapshot, and throws an <code>IvError</code> synchronously for an invalid theme. It is a subpath of its own, so <code>theme.js</code> and the default bundle did not grow.</p>
<pre><code>import &#123; setThemeWithTransition &#125; from "@intervolutions/ivolt/theme-transition";
button.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; setThemeWithTransition("dark", &#123; persist: true &#125;));</code></pre>
    <p><button class="iv-button iv-button--secondary iv-button--sm" type="button" data-theme-transition hidden>Try it: switch this page's theme</button></p>

    <h2>Semantic colours</h2>
    <div class="docs-scroller">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Light</th><th>Dark</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{semantic.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td><code>{v.value.light}</code></td><td><code>{v.value.dark}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Contrast targets: text 4.5:1, controls and focus 3:1 on their surfaces. The values above were checked against the WCAG formula; results are re-measured on each release.</p>

    <h2>The expressive layer</h2>
    <p>Six tokens added in v0.6. They name the recipes the polish pass had repeated by hand across a dozen modules — a shadow, a hover tint, a brand edge, a focus halo and two letter spacings — so every module now reads the same value from one place. Nothing changed visually when they landed: they are names for what was already drawn.</p>
    <div class="docs-scroller">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Light</th><th>Dark</th><th>Where it is used</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-shadow-ambient</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-shadow-ambient", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-shadow-ambient", "dark")}</code></td><td>Resting elevation of card, wrapped table, alert, accordion and button. One token instead of five recipes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-color-hover-surface</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-color-hover-surface", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-color-hover-surface", "dark")}</code></td><td>Pointer tint on card, dialog and drawer footers, tabs and the picker field. Table and menu rows keep the brand tint of <code>primary-subtle</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-color-primary-border</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-color-primary-border", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-color-primary-border", "dark")}</code></td><td>Brand outline: secondary button, field hover, pagination, and anywhere an edge should read as the brand.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-focus-halo</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-focus-halo", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-focus-halo", "dark")}</code></td><td>The glow that accompanies the focus ring on fields, buttons, pagination, breadcrumbs, stepper markers and the navbar toggle. Always next to the outline, never instead of it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-tracking-tight</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-tracking-tight", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-tracking-tight", "dark")}</code></td><td>Letter spacing of card, dialog, drawer and stat titles.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--iv-tracking-caps</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-tracking-caps", "light")}</code></td><td><code>{val("--iv-tracking-caps", "dark")}</code></td><td>Letter spacing of small caps: table headers, fieldset legends, megamenu and palette headings, stat labels.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The two colours also appear in the table above, because they are semantic colours like any other. All six are plain custom properties: override them on <code>:root</code> or on a scope and every component that reads them follows.</p>
    <h2>Scales</h2>
    <div class="docs-scroller">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Tokens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Space</td><td><code>--iv-space-0 … 16</code> (0.25rem steps up to 1.5rem, then 2, 2.5, 3, 4rem)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Type</td><td><code>--iv-text-xs … 4xl</code>, <code>--iv-leading-*</code>, <code>--iv-weight-*</code>, <code>--iv-font-sans|mono</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shape</td><td><code>--iv-radius-sm|md|lg|full</code>, <code>--iv-border-width</code>, <code>--iv-shadow-1|2|3</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Focus</td><td><code>--iv-focus-width</code>, <code>--iv-focus-offset</code>, <code>--iv-color-focus</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motion</td><td><code>--iv-motion-fast|base|slow</code> (120/180/220 ms; 0 ms under reduced motion), <code>--iv-ease-out</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Layers</td><td><code>--iv-z-dropdown|drawer|dialog|toast</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Measures</td><td><code>--iv-measure</code> (68ch), <code>--iv-container-max</code> (75rem), <code>--iv-content-max</code> (90rem)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Breakpoints</td><td>sm 30em · md 48em · lg 64em · xl 80em · 2xl 90em. Resolved at build time; not available as custom properties inside media queries.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
